<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akhetaten</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amarna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akhetaten, known today as Amarna, was the capital city founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a> in the fourteenth century BCE. It was abandoned soon after his death. The site is famous for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, diplomatic correspondence in Akkadian between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and powers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, which makes it important for the history of several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akhetaten.jpg" alt="Akhetaten" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Markh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Small_aten_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ebla exchanged messengers and gifts with Mari, Nagar, Kish and other courts, a diplomatic system centuries older than the better-known one of the <b>Amarna</b> age.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>He raised the Aten above all other gods, suppressed the Amun cult, and moved the capital to <b>Akhetaten</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The diplomatic correspondence found at <b>Amarna</b> and Hattusa documents great-power relations among the Hittites, Egypt, Babylon, Assyria and Mitanni, conducted in Akkadian as the Bronze Age international language.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Amarna</b> age, around 1350 BCE, the Lebanese coastal city-states fought for dominance as Egypt declined: the Akkar Plain rebelled with Hittite support to become the kingdom of Amurru, the Mitanni Empire that had dominated Syria fell apart under the campaigns of Suppiluliuma I of Hatti, and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…1350 BCE the pharaoh Amenhotep IV broke with this tradition: renaming himself Akhenaten, he exalted the Aten, the visible disc of the sun, above all other gods, closed or neglected their temples, had the name of Amun hacked out of monuments and founded a new capital at <b>Amarna</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Rulers who spoke Egyptian, Hittite, Hurrian or Canaanite languages corresponded in Akkadian, and scribal schools across the region, from Hattusa in Anatolia to Ugarit on the Syrian coast and <b>Amarna</b> in Egypt, trained scribes to read and write it.</blockquote><a href="/language/akkadian">Akkadian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
